Chapter: SmpProc0Pinned

 Proc 0 stays on the boot processor, and the machine is genuinely
 multi-core while it does.

 Slot 0 is the program the machine booted. It owns the boot stack and the
 main heap, and three separate places in the scheduler make sure no other
 core can take it: __idle_dispatch starts each core's scan at its own id
 and wraps to 1, so an application processor never reaches slot 0, and both
 preemption scans skip slot 0 outright when the claiming core is not the
 boot processor. Proc 0's affinity field says the same thing -- it names
 core 0 -- where it used to say -1, "any core", which was a licence nothing
 in the scheduler honoured.

 The evidence is the core stamp. Whichever core wins the claim for a
 process writes its own id into that process's proc-core field, so the
 field is the claiming core's signature. Slot 0's must still read 0 after a
 run in which every other core was demonstrably busy.

 "Demonstrably busy" is what stops this being a test that cannot fail. Zero
 is also the value the field is initialised to, so slot 0 reading zero
 proves nothing on its own -- it would read zero on one core, on a machine
 where no application processor ever executed an instruction, and on a
 machine where the core stamp was never written at all. Three further
 readings close each of those:

   cell 36200 counts every claim made by a core whose id is not zero, so
   above zero means an application processor really did take a process out
   of the table and run it;

   cell 36216 counts every timer interrupt taken on such a core, so above
   zero means one really was preempted rather than merely started;

   and the largest core stamp across the other fifteen slots must itself be
   above zero, which is the field this test reads on slot 0, proven able to
   hold a value other than the one slot 0 is required to hold.

 The control was run rather than assumed, and its shape is worth writing
 down because it is not the shape anyone would predict. A compiler was
 built with __idle_dispatch's scan-wrap forced to slot 0 AND proc 0's
 affinity put back to -1 -- both are needed, because either one alone
 refuses an application processor on its own, which is itself the finding
 that the affinity field is now load-bearing rather than decorative. The
 guest built by that compiler runs for seven minutes without printing a
 line and without faulting, and had to be killed. It does not report PROC 0
 MIGRATED; it never reports anything.

 That is the failure the guards' own prose predicts. An application
 processor that claims slot 0 resumes the booted program on the boot stack
 and strands it RUNNING on a core that is not its own, so the waits never
 return and the test is a runtime failure rather than a wrong answer. A
 shorter spin was tried first and passed cleanly on the broken build: proc
 0 is READY only in the instant between a child's exit waking it and the
 boot processor picking it up again, so a guest that finishes inside a
 couple of scheduling ticks never opens the window at all. The window is a
 function of how long the run is, which is the second reason the spin is
 the length it is.

 It needs a `.smp` sidecar; on one core there is nothing to migrate to and
 the two counters are both zero by construction.

Section: Body

  spin : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  spin (n) (acc) = if n == 0 then acc else spin (n - 1) (acc + 1)

 The spin is smp-preempt's, digit for digit, and for smp-preempt's reason:
 a scheduling tick arrives every few tens of milliseconds, so a child that
 finishes inside one is never a candidate for preemption on any core and
 the second reading below comes back NO AP WAS PREEMPTED while everything
 underneath works. The first cut of this test used a tenth of this and did
 exactly that. A shorter spin does not make the test faster, it makes it a
 test of the clock.

  child-body : Integer -> Integer
  child-body (x) = spin 1000000000 0

 The largest core stamp over slots 1..15. proc-table-base is 20480, an
 entry is 256 bytes, and the core field sits 8 bytes in.

  max-child-core : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  max-child-core (i) (best) =
   if i >= 16 then best
   else let c = atomic-load (20480 + i * 256 + 8)
   in max-child-core (i + 1) (if c > best then c else best)

  opening : [Console, Concurrent] Nothing = act
    a <- process-spawn (\x -> child-body x)
    b <- process-spawn (\x -> child-body x)
    c <- process-spawn (\x -> child-body x)
    d <- process-spawn (\x -> child-body x)
    e <- process-spawn (\x -> child-body x)
    f <- process-spawn (\x -> child-body x)
    wa <- process-wait a
    wb <- process-wait b
    wc <- process-wait c
    wd <- process-wait d
    we <- process-wait e
    wf <- process-wait f
    print-line-uni (if atomic-load 36200 > 0
                    then "an ap claimed and ran a process"
                    else "NO AP RAN A PROCESS")
    print-line-uni (if atomic-load 36216 > 0
                    then "an ap was preempted"
                    else "NO AP WAS PREEMPTED")
    print-line-uni (if max-child-core 1 0 > 0
                    then "a core stamp above zero was written"
                    else "NO CORE STAMP ABOVE ZERO")
    print-line-uni (if atomic-load 20488 == 0
                    then "proc 0 stayed on the boot processor"
                    else "PROC 0 MIGRATED")
  end
